{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The stated relation is 'abstraction' of a digit-usage function D(n), but the only mechanism offered is a fixed numeric walkthrough ('dividing the remainder by 3 and summing the parts') tied to the single budget 792 and the single result 300. There is no variable-binding rule, piecewise definition, or parameterization over n that would causally produce D(n) for any input other than 792; the target merely summarizes SRC_002's instance computation. Without such a generalizing mechanism, the 'abstraction' claim collapses into a restatement of the shared arithmetic, not a substantive relation.","target":"55508ef1cc12a7baab6f7b88f259f64106b90b1b16a882d2d09ff908cc1879f4"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target frames the relation as 'SRC_006 depends on SRC_004' for the result 300, but the offered causal basis is only that both evaluate the expression 9+90×2+(792−189)/3. That shared expression does not establish a dependence of SRC_006 on SRC_004; two artifacts computing the same closed-form quantity can do so independently. The only differentiating claim (SRC_006 'corrects' SRC_004 by retaining the initial 99 pages) presupposes that SRC_004 omits those pages, which is not evidenced in the summary—so the corrective dependence mechanism is asserted, not derived from SRC_004's content.","target":"8055db21e10954dfdb1360a204fc2acc1046f8eb7f269da708e3881d1ad8ea44"},{"attack":false,"case":"The 'reduces to' relation is substantively specified: SRC_005's closed-form D(n)=9+180+(n−99)×3 is identified as an algebraic simplification of SRC_003's explicit step sequence (9+180=189; 792−189=603; 603/3=201; 99+201=300), and a concrete refuting condition is given (SRC_005's LHS not being D(n)=792, or the 99+201 total failing additivity). The causal mechanism—the substitution of the stepwise computation into the compact formula—is present and target-specific, so no independent fault is shown.","target":"b46bc9dc445ceb29bef71e914a557e6b88ad905710faeb196fdf320195b5